<title>CZECH REPUBLIC: CZECH PRAZSKE PIVOVARY Q1 CZK 107MLN LOSS.</title>
<headline>CZECH PRAZSKE PIVOVARY Q1 CZK 107MLN LOSS.</headline>
<dateline>PRAGUE 1997-05-09</dateline>
<text>
<p>Czech brewer Prazske Pivovary a.s., 55 percent owned by Britain's Bass PLC , said on Friday it posted a loss of 107.24 million crowns in the first quarter of 1997.</p>
<p>Comparatives are unavailable because the figures are consolidated and the brewery recently merged into a new legal entity after the acquisition last year of two regional breweries.  </p>
<p>Results for three month period ending March 31, 1997 (in</p>
<p>millions of crowns).</p>
<p>				  Q1 1997	  vs     Q1 1996</p>
<p> Gross profit		-107.236(loss)	     n/a</p>
<p> Net profit		  -107.236(loss)	     n/a</p>
<p> Operating profit	   -54.372 (loss)	    n/a</p>
<p> Write offs		    42.074		     n/a</p>
<p>Sales of own goods</p>
<p> and services		 430.659		     n/a</p>
<p> Production		   442.184		     n/a</p>
<p> Basic capital	    1,717.474		     n/a</p>
<p> Assets		     3,388.285		     n/a</p>
<p>NOTE: The company is the third largest Czech brewing group by</p>
<p>market share. Bass, the largest shareholder with 55 percent,</p>
<p>also has a 33 percent stake in the second biggest Czech brewer,</p>
<p>Radegast a.s. .</p>
<p>-- Robin Shepherd, Prague Newsroom, 420-2-2423-0003</p>
</text>
